1 Durable Buffers
*****************

GNU Emacs keeps unnamed buffers (‘*scratch*’) in memory only: kill the
process at the wrong moment and the work is gone.  Ymacs replaces that
law outright.

   *Every Ymacs buffer is either a file or a row in the store.*  There
is no third state and no memory-only buffer.

   • A buffer visiting a file is saved to that file, exactly as in Emacs
     (*note (emacs)Save Commands::).  Its crash-safety draft lives in
     the store's ‘drafts’ table, not in loose files.
   • A buffer with no file (scratchpads, captured output, the palette's
     internal state) is a row in the ‘buffers’ table of the live
     profile's store.  The write happens at creation and at every
     command — ‘C-x C-s’ is unnecessary for unnamed buffers because _the
     write is the save_.

   The durability choke point is the command layer: every command's
effect on the current buffer is in the store before the next keystroke
is read.  That is what makes macros replay headless and what makes work
impossible to lose.


