2 Profiles
**********

Ymacs has “profiles”, in the sense of a web browser's tab sets (*note
(ychrome)Profiles::): a named window over a set of buffers.  The profile
named ‘default’ exists from first boot.

‘M-x sidebar/profiles <RET>’
     Open the Profiles sidebar: every profile with the live one marked,
     and a field to create a new one.  Selecting a row switches profile.
‘M-x ymacs-switch-profile <RET> NAME <RET>’
     Switch profile by name.

   Switching a profile persists the current profile's open buffer set,
empties the window, and loads the target's — the daemon never stops, no
buffer is deleted, and the other profile's buffers are untouched.
Buffers of one profile are never the business of another.

   *ymacs is perpetual.*  A GUI restart, a profile switch, an ssh drop —
none of them end the daemon and none of them lose work.  The one way
ymacs ends is explicit:

‘M-x save-buffers-kill-ymacs <RET>’
     Persist every buffer and the session, then stop the daemon.


