5 The Command Palette
*********************

In GNU Emacs, ‘M-x’ reads a command name in the echo area, and
completion candidates appear in a separate ‘*Completions*’ window.
Ymacs keeps the reading machinery exactly where Emacs keeps it: the
minibuffer is a state machine in the editor's core, feeding the command
layer, and every command still runs through ‘command-execute’, the one
choke point.  What changes is only the _surface_ the read is drawn on.
Ymacs renders a read as a “command palette”: one field and its results
in a single panel, floating over the buffer.  The field is the top edge
of the results, not a control above them, so the palette is one object
on screen.

   Because the palette is only a view, what holds for the minibuffer in
Emacs holds here too.  Keyboard macros that invoke commands through
‘M-x’ record the invocation, never the typing, and replay headlessly
with the palette nowhere in sight (*note (emacs)Keyboard Macros::).
Re-shaping the palette cannot break a recorded macro.

‘M-x’
     Read a command name in the palette and run it.  Type part of a
     name; every candidate containing what you typed (all the
     space-separated parts, in any position) is offered.
‘<TAB>’
     Complete to the current candidate's text.
‘C-n, C-p’
     Move down and up the candidate list.  The arrow keys and mouse
     clicks do the same on the palette itself.
‘<RET>’
     Run the current candidate, or collect it as the answer to the read.
‘C-g’
     Quit the read: nothing runs, nothing changes.

   The palette is “intelligent” in the omnibox sense.  Commands you have
run recently rank first; the top candidate that extends what you typed
appears in the field itself with its tail selected, so typing is never
interrupted -- continue typing to refine it, or take it with ‘TAB’ or
<RET>.  Each row also shows, at its right edge, the command's key
binding when it has one (‘C-x C-f’ beside ‘find-file’), and beside the
name a short description drawn from the command's own documentation
string.

   Reads that collect an argument for a command (‘C-x C-f’'s "Find file:
", ‘C-x b’'s buffer name, and every other ‘interactive’ spec) use the
same palette.  There the read is “lenient”: what you typed is itself
offered as a candidate, so ‘C-x b’ ‘foo’ <RET> reads ‘foo’ even when no
buffer has that name, exactly as Emacs hands ‘foo’ to the command.  Only
the command-name read of ‘M-x’ is “strict”: a name that is not a command
draws ‘[No match]’ and the read stays open, as in Emacs.

   The candidate window slides so the selected row is always on screen;
the full list is as long as the read's collection, not the window.


